Use the quadratic formula with \(a=1\), \(b=4\), and \(c=1\):
\[
\begin{aligned}
x &= \frac{-4\pm\sqrt{4^2-4(1)(1)}}{2}\\
&= \frac{-4\pm\sqrt{12}}{2}\\
&= \frac{-4\pm2\sqrt{3}}{2}\\
&= -2\pm\sqrt{3}
\end{aligned}
\]
Therefore the exact solution set is \(x=-2\pm\sqrt{3}\). The option \(2\pm\sqrt{3}\) has the wrong sign for the rational part.

Use the quadratic formula with \(a=1\), \(b=-2\), and \(c=-4\):
\[
\begin{aligned}
x &= \frac{2\pm\sqrt{(-2)^2-4(1)(-4)}}{2}\\
&= \frac{2\pm\sqrt{20}}{2}\\
&= \frac{2\pm2\sqrt{5}}{2}\\
&= 1\pm\sqrt{5}
\end{aligned}
\]
Therefore the exact solution set is \(x=1\pm\sqrt{5}\). The option \(1\pm\sqrt{3}\) comes from an incorrect discriminant.
